WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.138
PQC for TLS and web services prepares web communication for a future where some current public-key algorithms may no longer provide enough protection.

2
00:00:10.138 --> 00:00:18.929
TLS protects browsers, APIs, service-to-service calls, mobile backends, gateways, SaaS platforms, and internal applications.

3
00:00:18.929 --> 00:00:23.047
The migration challenge is not simply choosing an algorithm.

4
00:00:23.047 --> 00:00:30.888
It is understanding how handshake roles, certificates, clients, libraries, proxies, and operations fit together.

5
00:00:30.888 --> 00:00:35.957
The most important technical distinction is between cryptographic roles.

6
00:00:35.957 --> 00:00:40.551
Key establishment helps create shared key material for the session.

7
00:00:40.551 --> 00:00:45.461
Digital signatures help authenticate identities and verify integrity.

8
00:00:45.461 --> 00:00:50.451
Symmetric encryption protects the application data after the handshake.

9
00:00:50.451 --> 00:00:55.757
Post-quantum KEMs, such as ML-KEM conceptually, relate to key establishment.

10
00:00:55.757 --> 00:01:04.311
Post-quantum signature algorithms, such as ML-DSA and SLH-DSA conceptually, relate to authentication and signing use cases.

11
00:01:04.311 --> 00:01:12.389
Hybrid key exchange can help during the transition, but it should be standards-based, tested, monitored, and governed.

12
00:01:12.389 --> 00:01:17.379
It does not remove the need for compatibility work or downgrade awareness.

13
00:01:17.379 --> 00:01:30.209
Certificate and PKI migration is its own ecosystem challenge involving browsers, operating systems, certificate authorities, trust stores, libraries, devices, and enterprise products.

14
00:01:30.209 --> 00:01:34.882
Web trust changes only safely when the whole validation path is ready.

15
00:01:34.882 --> 00:01:39.000
Strong readiness starts with inventory and prioritization.

16
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:51.356
Find TLS endpoints, classify data, identify owners, map clients, track libraries and vendors, test in labs, deploy in stages, monitor production signals, and prepare rollback plans.

17
00:01:51.356 --> 00:01:54.207
The goal is not panic-driven replacement.

18
00:01:54.207 --> 00:02:04.820
The goal is practical crypto agility for critical web services, so organizations can adapt deliberately as standards, libraries, and vendor support mature.
